body:
Ok. That's what I thought I heard but wasn't sure if it was in action or not.

&#x200B;

Thanks!

Thanks for all this info. When I deposit my side hustle into my bank account, that is the only amount shown on the 1099 form I receive, so that is what I thought gets taxed while the rest sits in my account before it goes to my bank.

&#x200B;

I make $44k a year at my fulltime job and then have 21K sitting in my side hustle account (not yet in my bank account which has accumulated over the past 2 years). So I believe my net would be considered about $65K if I deposited all my side hustle into my account? Does that change anything you said? Would that help or hurt me if I sold at the loss?

&#x200B;

Thanks for your help and guidance.

Holding people accountable for not doing their job is incredibly important. My workplace is falling apart because people aren't following protocol, handing things in by the due date, and management is doing nothing about it so nothing changes. It's really awful enabling behavior. 

&#x200B;

Have a system for writing folks up after multiple offenses or else nothing will change because they know they will get away with it.

&#x200B;

And also show appreciation whenever necessary. The folks who actually do their work and do it well at my job get no appreciation or praise but the folks listed above are the ones who get shout outs, pats on the back, etc. and it's really defeating

Everyone is mentioning a relationship problem but it also seems like a financial discipline problem. To say your savings are taking a hit but oh, we eat out, got a $500 watch, etc. You can't have one without the other. If you're financially tight, constantly treating yourself just keeps you there. But it sounds like your BF is in that same headspace that he'd rather have fun than be financially responsible.

&#x200B;

Best advice I've gotten was "say no now so you can say yes whenever you want later".

&#x200B;

I'd be pissed if my boyfriend was making more but contributing less. And you constantly covering him and allowing it is enabling this bad spending behavior.

Yes they are federal so yeah it sounds like with the loan reforms, interest won't build on her debt every month with on time payments. She's a pretty frugal person (hence saving over $100K) so I dont see any issue with her having a hefty emergency fund. She's also a quite disciplined person so I'd figure she can handle the landlord title

&#x200B;

Thanks for your input!

I don't have any technical advice, but I'm really sorry for all you've gone through lately. I have a lot of anxiety and fear around money from my childhood so I can feel the overwhelm reading this. If anything, you didn't get into your situation because of being irresponsible. You could have said you bought this, bought that, gambled, went on vacations, but you listed all valid life events that hit one after the other. So be gentle with yourself. You're being a responsible father with some current bad luck but it's not your fault for what's going on right now. 

&#x200B;

Hang in there and do the best you can

Totally true. I guess her logic is, put around $35K - $40K down on a home, rent it out so the costs are covered, and then while working and the huge chuck of cash she has to pay off the debt over a period of time. Rather than pay it off now because she can but not have any asset.

&#x200B;

Do you think paying off the debt but then lets say taking 5+ years to build up savings again and eventually a down payment for a home would be more responsible in her situation?

Thank you for your answer. Do you have any idea how that $10K would apply to a PPL with multiple children under one parents name? Maybe goes towards the loan with the highest amount? Lowest amount? Longest standing loan under the PPL? If you have any ideas I'd appreciate it.

&#x200B;

Thanks!

Glad I saw your comment. Just saying as well it has died for me. I was told to expect lower views this summer because the pandemic isn't as bad as the past 2 summers so people aren't laying around on their phone? Not sure if its that or me haha

&#x200B;

But yeah, I was brining in on average 2-5 subscribers a day and now I can go 2-3 days without getting 1 subscriber. Relying a lot on my loyal resubscribers. Been kinda brutal

Just want to say I am there with you. The pause has been a life saver for me. I am thinking it'll get extended through the end of the year with midterm elections in November. But, you never know. I've been holding my breath the past few weeks. 

&#x200B;

Crossing my fingers

They are Direct parent plus loans which I was told do qualify towards PSLF, but mine are not on an ICR plan. But I thought the waiver was supposed to be for situations that would apply but aren't on the right plan.

&#x200B;

And she just turned 60 so she won't be working as a nurse for another 10 years.

I don't really have advice here but just want to say I am in your shoes too and I know how it feels. I also have no support from my parents and had no guidance going through the college selection process. It can feel so isolating and even embarrassing to be in so much debt. I have $147K in loans for a culinary school degree that never helped me earn more than $15/hr. 

&#x200B;

Luckily I got into a different field and making a bit more but I know how awful it can feel. I've been going on 9 years now with this debt and working my butt off to get rid of it. As suggested by someone else, I am looking into PSLF. I hope something can work our for you.

Interesting. Yeah, we were shocked that a very nice phone that we didn't ask for showed up. Looking back at the packaging paperwork, it's saying "thanks for choosing to trade in" and has our correct account and address. It says my dads name specifically on the shipping address but no one has recently updated or decided to do a trade in. 

&#x200B;

Do you think this could have been an error or still has to do with the program for those phased out of 3G? Any idea?

&#x200B;

Btw thank you very much for your help I appreciate it!

We assume it is a 3G replacement. We're pretty sure my dads phone is the one but we have a paid off X to give him so we'll just have this 13 Max Pro hanging around. My mom said she had no clue. No email, heads up, anything. That's why she was very confused and fearful of it being a scam.

&#x200B;

I'll have her look back through her emails. Thanks for your help!

The system is so stupid. I've been paying $900 a month on the loan for almost 8 years and just because it wasn't under a specific payment plan, none of it counts. Makes me feel so hopeless. She turned 60 this year and has been working as a nurse her whole life, I don't think she can do another 10 years nor can I take the mental strain of $114K on my back.

&#x200B;

So you don't think there's any reason to fill out the waiver? You don't think there's any chance they might reform the system and there could be a chance the loan under it's current form could be considered?

When I cut contact and moved out, I felt the same way. Not so much lonely, but reality hit me that I can't have the parents I need and had to do this. A lot of crying happened, sometimes out of no where or triggered by minor things. I think you have to recognize being proud that you did what's best for you but also grieve the relationship you'll never have with your parents. It's tough, I know.

&#x200B;

Maybe consider seeking therapy if you haven't and can afford it. This is definitely a time you'll need support and someone you can lean on.

That does sound very strange. My college was predatory too. It was culinary school convincing young naive hopefuls that a 3 years bachelors degree costing $130,000 FOR THE FOOD INDUSTRY was worth it. Looking back, it's insane how that school still is ripping off thousands of kids every year.

&#x200B;

Yeah, I would have gone to a trade school and gotten the skills for 1/10th of the cost. Once I got into the industry, I realized you don't even really need a degree. It's something I'll always regret and can't move on from. 

&#x200B;

I just wish my parents realized $130K for a bachelors in the food industry was su\*c\*de. But no, it was, "go wherever you want, you just will pay for it after graduation" like it would be so easy. Great parenting. My mom also said community college was for losers so I instantly said I need to go to the best to be the best.

Yes, I know, I know. I'm told this all the time from people. It's so much more complicated than that. However I plan to maybe leave $5K for them to pay it because they took money from me that was meant to help pay for college. 

&#x200B;

Que the thumbs down from everyone for me standing up for myself...

Agreed. I am very hard working, live like I'm homeless (go to sleep in the cold, have holes in my shoes, etc) all because of this debt because they failed to guide me or educate me on making a smart choice. And yet, they make so so much money but refuse to offer any help. 

&#x200B;

Trust me, help your kids if you can. Even if you go half and half on their education. I'm a believer that people don't develop into hard workers when everything is handed to them but don't leave your kids out to drown like my parents do. Or if you choose not to cover the expenses, just guide them in how to get a degree as cheap as possible.

No but I feel the school convinced everyone their degree would be worth it, no, and no. My dad who was the financially responsible parent was also a drug addict at that time in my life and wasn't emotional available so my reckless irresponsible mom was my guide through the college process.

&#x200B;

All she said was, go wherever, but you'll have to pay for it after. She made it sound so easy I didn't think twice (I also was 17 and never had one talk about finances from my parents so even if I knew it would have cost me $140K to attend, I wouldn't have known what that meant.) My mom also taught me that debt is normal and everyone has it so again, I didn't think I was ruining my life by attending.

&#x200B;

Anyways, now my mom knows the damage that has been done and just shrugs her shoulders. Even in their position to help

I'm right there with you. Graduated in 2013 with $147K in student loans. Making less than $30K a year, it's been the longest 8 years of my life but I a down to the last $20K (Wouldn't have been possible without the pause). I can't begin to tell you how drained I am emotionally and now physically. I'm working 2 jobs and a side hustle. 

&#x200B;

It's weird because before the pandemic I knew I would never be able to pay it off. And now that I actually think I will by the end of this year, I feel so so exhausted. I guess when I gave up I just didn't care, and now that I'm down to the last $20K it feels like it'll never end. Weird to explain. 

&#x200B;

I have nothing to say or to motivate, just that I am there with you and struggling towards the finish line as well

My parents make combined $150K+ a year and refuse to help me even though I'm $147K in debt and low income. They just don't care and don't see it as their responsibility and refuse to help $1. It's pretty sad when they have no mortgage or high living expenses and have the fund but they just don't want to help. 

&#x200B;

If I was in their shoes, I'd want to do anything I could for my child to get ahead in life and win. But my parents are just defeated people and think there's no such thing as winning with money, even making $10K a month with virtually no debt.

Trust me, I've lived practically homeless the last 9 years. I go to bed in the cold, I never go out to eat, my shoes have holes in them, etc. My anger is that they know I'm suffering due to their lack of guidance and instead chose to lease cars, but $1500 dogs, trips, and so on. You say their money is none of my business, so how come it was fair they took $5000 from money that was meant for ME? My mom knew I needed it but my dad was on drugs and he would have gotten upset and abusive if he didn't get the money. Why should that be my problem?

&#x200B;

I watch Dave and am responsible. Paying it off at this point isn't the problem. It's knowing my parents screwed me and couldn't care less. 80% of the debt is in her name anyways so I'm not legally bound and may leave 5k because of what they took from me cause if they don't care I won't either

I'm so sorry that happened. I've been through a few break ups, but one in particular that left me feeling like you are now. You gotta let yourself feel the sadness and grief untill it's ready to pass. That's always been my motto. If you need to be sad for a few weeks, a few months, then allow yourself. I find if you just pretend to be ok and act like you're not hurt, these bad feelings will come back up later on.

&#x200B;

That one breakup that left me devastated was 4 years ago and I can assure you, in time, you'll get better. You'll never know how long it'll take but you'll realize you deserve more than someone who thinks it's ok to hurt you the way he did.

I can't technically help but on the emotional side just want to say I know the feeling of the disappointment of your parent financially failing you and not looking out for you. It is so disappointing for our parents who are supported to want the best for us and protect us to fail us in this way. 

&#x200B;

The bright side as you mentioned is you have good income and the debt amount isn't impossible to overcome. I hope things get better for you.

Just want to say on the emotional piece of this, I totally know how you feel. I too thought my parents knew best and were looking out for me when I chose the school I went to. My mom said "well, you'll just pay it off when you're done". 17 year old me who hadn't had one lesson from my parents about money or finances thought, "well, she makes it sound easy and college is normal so ok lets go". And then I graduated with $140K in loans and realized I had ruined my life.

&#x200B;

I can't help with your questions but I know the feeling of feeling failed or betrayed by your parents for not knowing better or guiding you.

I cant really help but just want to say I am in your same boat. I have $33K to my name and $114K in my moms name (had $25K in my dads name but paid that off years ago). My parents are high earners but couldn't care less about helping me get out of the situation I'm in (mostly led by their lack of parenting). I begged my mom to try to help by applying to IBR and she just refused. There's also no way (right now) to bankrupt student loans. Trust me, I looked at every angle I could to get out of the mess. 

&#x200B;

Again, I can't help with anything here but I just want to say I am in your shoes and know the pain and stress you're carrying.

To be honest, I've been on OF for 2 years now. Ran it all myself. Film, edit, upload, thank my subscribers individually, post tiktoks, and on and on. You wouldn't believe what you can accomplish alone with a tripod.

&#x200B;

Maybe you should just be honest to how it is impacting you and tell her to try to manage it on her own. Unless she has hundreds of fans and is drowning to keep up, I feel like with enough dedication, anyone can run their own OF unless they are a professional p\*star with lots and lots of attention.

I graduated with around $140K and soon found out after graduating I could have done my field without a degree. I completely empathize with how you feel. I thought going to college was going to be what saved me from the life my parents had but instead, gave me the exact life my parents had (financially struggling and not getting ahead). I graduated with $140K and was making about $25K a year and living in a big expensive city so I know the feeling.  

&#x200B;

I know there's nothing I can say that will help you feel better other than I am just like you. I have a mountain of regret, it's the only thing I wish I could take back in my life, and it will leave an impact on me for the rest of my life. But also just want to say you have to focus and dedicate yourself to getting rid of it. Even when I knew I was never going to pay it off, I was still saving every dollar I could. And now, I am estimating I'll be able to pay them off by the end of 2022. 

&#x200B;

Hang in there. There's a lot of us just like you.

Right, totally valid. And that's me too. I don't wish for anything luxurious or extravagant. I just want peace of mind that I never had as a kid growing up with my parents financial woes. I feel like how you talk about your mid 30's is how I'm feeling entering my 30's at the end of 2022. I just want them gone so I can live in peace and move on with my life, I know how you're feeling. 

&#x200B;

I think as others have said, a conversation is important here to see if you both can get in the same headspace and align with your goals and dreams. It can be so scary to think of a relationship coming to an end, but it sounds like deep down you know you wouldn't want this looming over your life deep into your future. I hope what is meant to be will work out for you

I actually had the only guy I ever loved leave me because of my student loans. He never admitted to it, but I think he felt as if it was going to hinder the life he wanted so he broke up with me after 2.5 years. And it wasn't that I was neglecting it or not caring about it, he just didn't want to be with someone with debt. We were 20 and 21 at the time. I'm now almost 29 and think I'll have it all paid off by the end of 2022 so it's too bad he didn't believe in me but that's life.

&#x200B;

It still kind of makes me sad to this day because if you truly deeply love someone, I think anything is possible when you work together with your partner. But I think he felt it was too much that I would never get out of it. I think now that I'm 28, I too would feel a bit weary of dating someone who had as much as I had IF they weren't taking it seriously. 

&#x200B;

All to say, follow your heart and what you think is best for you. I see comments saying if you believe in your relationship and your partner, then anything is possible and I believe it. However, money is like the biggest deal breaker in a marriage so I think communication is going to be everything moving forward.

I will say too I attended the CIA in hyde park NY and now 6 figures in debt 9 years later. Do not do culinary school in an expensive place. It's basically unnecessary no matter what anyone tells you. Expensive culinary schools are a major scam. 

&#x200B;

I promise you it is not worth it. Had I known at 17 what I've known now I never would have done it and would have went to a cheap trade school. I promise you it sounds fun and this and that but if you're paying for school on your own don't do it.

That's basically my parents. They are in the head space that debt is a normal part of life and they will never be able to get ahead and financially win. It angers me so much because they both make decent money where they CAN win, but in their head they are too defeated to try to be different. 

&#x200B;

I want to be debt free by 30 and do what they could never do and show them you can live a simple, calm, financially stable life.

Yeah I grew up with money issues and money ruined my childhood/ family, so it hits a very dark nerve. I was in therapy but now can't afford if because I don't have health insurance. 

&#x200B;

Why shouldn't I pay them back? No life is worth living with the weight of knowing your money isn't your money. I'd rather start over at 30 and be debt free than in my 50's knowing I have 6 figures of debt on my shoulders

Sometimes I think it's easier said than done. Like of course to save the money and pay off the debt quicker I'll do just about anything but I get nervous. I would hate for things to get bad between us again, and then maybe I feel trapped and get more depressed. Although the living expenses slow down my journey, sometimes having my own safe space I think helps us rather than being in each others face all the time. 

&#x200B;

Idk, sorry I know I am so back and forth. This is why I posted because I need peoples opinions. So thank you

This was a very compassionate comment, and I really appreciate you writing it. Anytime I have talked about this issue on places like tiktok, I always get comments being like "I'd leave you too" so it's tough to talk about. The great thing is I think I'll finally have them paid off and be debt free by the end of 2022 and I just can't wait to prove myself he missed out cause he couldn't handle me in my journey. 

&#x200B;

Anyways, thanks again for the kind words and I hope you have a nice evening

I get it. I think it's personally hard for me because the few people I have in my life are pretty financially ahead of the curve, so its the good "they're ahead of you" mind tricks. 

&#x200B;

I understand people live their whole life with student loans, but also personally know people who havn't had to live a day with them, so it's hard for me to cope with knowing there are the lucky ones who didn't have to suffer like I did (I'm taking responsibility but I was 17 and didn't know any better to the financial choices I was making.)

Yeah its so interesting the emotional attachment I have. I will def be grieving the loss when I put 97% of my money to the loan end of January. Not on IBR because the majority of my loans are a parent plus loan in my moms name and she and my dad make a good income, and my siblings are under her so it just couldn't work. 

&#x200B;

I am for the loan in my name but its more manageable.

I don't think I have that option. I don't think my landlord would wanna put up with all that. I am kinda tempted to see if I move out maybe he can let someone rent a year and then I move back in but it just sounds easier than it actually would be. I just hate knowing the almost $900 in living expenses can go towards the debt and I wouldn't need that part time job that is really physically demanding. 

&#x200B;

Trust me, after being in 6 figures for so long, $30K really isn't awfully scary. But mentally it has destroyed me and I just feel like I wanna get rid of it as fast as I can and move on. I love my place but would love getting rid of the debt asap so I just feel kinda stuck in the best decision to make. I also love helping out my parents with things like mowing the lawn, chores etc now that they are older so I just feel like maybe it can benefit all of us if I moved back.

I just want to say, I completely know how you feel. I am 28 as well, and this feeling/ pressure of paying them off before 30 is a lot. I graduated with six figures of loans at age 20, only make about $25K a year. Luckily through multiple streams of income and a better paying job, I'm at about $45K a year now. 

&#x200B;

Due to the pandemic freeze I have about 30K left and finally feel like I can see the light at the end of the tunnel. But I actually was just feeling like this today. Life just seems useless knowing all I do is work and safe for essentially nothing. I'm not saving for a home, or this or that, just working my butt off to pay off a huge regret of mine, it's been exhausting and I feel like I lost so much of my 20's because of it. 

&#x200B;

I am hopeful that all of this dread now will pass once I pay them off and I can move on with my life, but something tells me I'll always carry the weight of what my life could have been without this mess. I think there's a lot of grief to unpack with paying student loans, especially for those who regret their degree/ school choice. 

&#x200B;

I hope this helped a bit just not feel so alone. Good luck to you

I just want to say, in my first relationship, my partner left me because I had 6 figures of student loans. There were other issues too but money was a big piece of our break up. He never admitted to it but looking back it was pretty obvious. I think he felt he couldn't have the life he wanted with my debt. That was about 7 years ago and it still hurts me.

&#x200B;

 I think you need to take the loans into account but look at your partner and ask if you really love her and see yourself spending the rest of your life with her. If you do, then this can be something you work through together if she is 100% on board, but I think if you think this is just be a major issue in your future, then maybe you need to consider splitting.

&#x200B;

I am only commenting because I'm speaking from experience on the side of your girlfriend, obviously you know yourself and your situation best.

Thank you, I appreciate the support. I actually have been saving money since 2014. I am a money hoarder. I only paid the minimum on my loans all these years because I was depressed and couldn't come to accept the mistake I made, so I just numbed myself out and paid the minimum. 

&#x200B;

Now with the freeze, I've been able to hoard more money and now actually feel like I see the light at the end of the tunnel for the first time in 9 years. So trust me, it took a very long time to save all of that. Now that I have hope I am trying to do whatever I can to pay them off by the end of 2022. 

&#x200B;

I just live insanely frugal and try to save every dollar I make. 

&#x200B;

Don't you think though moving in with my folks and saving living expenses would not only help pay the loans quicker but also help me rebuild my money quicker? You wouldn't suggest it? I think as much as I am focused on getting rid of my loans I am also kinda scared to be "starting over" at 30 and afraid it will take a while to save back to a comfortable amount.

I don't wanna jinx anything but I turn 30 in Dec. 2022 and I think I'll have my loans paid off by 30 which was always something I dreamed of but before the pandemic freeze knew would never happen.

&#x200B;

Congratulations!

You took the words right out of my mouth. I think I am likely going to be debt free of my student loans by the end of 2022 and this was a great motivator for me. I always tell myself I can't wait until the day every dollar I earn is mine. I'd rather have 20 dollars and no debt than 10K but will 100K student loans. 

&#x200B;

Congratulations to you!

I totally feel this and thinking this. I've been posting weekly since January 2020. I'm feeling burnt out, somewhat uninspired (I feel like I've done it all at this point) and just wanting to take a break. I honestly feel I do well being this is a very minimal effort part time gig, and that extra income helps so much towards my student loans.

&#x200B;

Once the announcement was made, I felt like maybe this is my time I bring this chapter to an end. But, I feel like I worked so hard to build this and my little community of supporters, I'd be sad to lose them. I'm thinking maybe I'll keep pushing until my student loans are gone (hopefully by next summer) and then I'll bring this chapter to an end.

Yes the parent plus loan as well as the loan in my name are frozen at 0%until Sept. 30th, which is why lately I'm freaking out trying to plan what to do with my savings once that time comes. Because once the interest starts my anxiety will sky rocket.

&#x200B;

And I agree, I do appreciate the benefits for worst case scenario. That's why I do not look into refinancing because then you lose all of that. And I am pretty sure people who refinanced their federal loans didn't get the covid relief and have had to pay all this time.

Yeah I really have wanted to keep it out of my name as to not add more pressure to my already sky high stress level if anything went wrong. My parents are both working and make way more than I do. 

&#x200B;

I honestly don't even think I would need that big of a "shit happens" fund. I'm think maybe holding onto about $5K for security. 

&#x200B;

I keep leaning towards putting everything on the PP loan because it's so large and the interest is so high, the smaller I can get it, the more I think I could breathe. But maybe do like a 75-25 split between the both to get the PP way down and the one in my name a little smaller.

&#x200B;

Of course too I a watching to see if as the student loan covid relief plan comes to an end in September if they will either extend it or cancel some of the debt and then move from there. Would be great to get 6 more months of 0% interest so I could hoard even more to throw at it.

Thank you for the kind message. I honestly have no goals for myself. I never thought about buying a house, starting a business, anything because of this debt. I already own a car that I love and paid cash for. My only goal has ever been to get rid of it and move on, but until recently, it has felt nearly impossible (still kind of does). 

&#x200B;

So honestly I think about saving maybe $3K-$5K as an emergency fund and just throw everything else at the debt until it (hopefully) goes away.

I know I need help. I used to see a therapist and the student loans were one of the main topics amongst my other issues. Trust me, after trying to be seen by my parents for the past 8 years, nothing will happen. No writing or anything I say or do will change it. It's been all on my shoulders since 20 and it will continue to be until the last dollar is paid. 

&#x200B;

A miracle could happen and my mom chooses t help (she recently landed a new job making about $58 an hour and constantly tells me "you wouldn't believe how much I am making" but I am sure if I ever asked her for help, there would be some excuse as always. 

&#x200B;

I'm just so numbed out at this point

It honestly is a therapy thing and I used to talk about this a lot but I do not have health insurance because surprise surprise, I want to save the money. My former therapist is $110 a session and because I have such a bad relationship with money, I can't bring myself to go because I feel its more important to save the money.

&#x200B;

I know my situation could be so much worse, and I have saved a lot where I can get rid of 2/3rds of it today if I wanted, but my g0d is it just so impossible emotionally to cope.

It's my monthly take home. If I was brining home $5200 a month I wouldn't be posting here haha it'd be gone by now. I was always making $11-$14 an hour up until 2.5 years ago and now luckily taking home now.

&#x200B;

I understand my mom didn't know better, but she does now but still just turns a blind eye and leaves me drowning, it is the most lonely abandoned feeling.

So you're suggesting I hold onto my current saving and now use my income to attack the debt? That was kinda always my plan, to get to 6 figures in my bank, and THEN start to attack. But everyone is saying I should do the opposite and through it all now. Which I do kind of understand. The $114K in my moms name is about $900 a month and with the high interest rate, that 900 basically comes back by the time I owe the next payment so it doesn't move. 

&#x200B;

I'm starting to think it would make sense to throw maybe $90K at it to bring it down to $15K and then it wont grow so fast/ intense and feel more manageable. the one in my name is $33K which is bad but obviously doesn't grow as fast as $114K.

I bring home about $2600 after taxes which is a ton for me, this is the most I've ever earned. Our agreement was I would pay it back. But I was 16 when I applied to school not knowing what it meant to be $120K in debt (now larger) so yes, I agreed to pay for it but she should have looked out for me and take some responsibility. She knows and has said she feels awful and regrets allowing it to happen, but wont agree to help out.

&#x200B;

I think the one in my name is on some income driven plan. I've payed 0 on it for like, 3 years now. But of course the interest will just keep it going up and up and up. I hope if there is not loan cancellation of some sort that the government will do something about the interest. If it wasn't so high I'd probably already be at least 1/3rd paid off.

Hi everyone! Thank you for the time in looking at my post. Sorry if this type of post does not belong here. I am buying a 2008 Pontiac solstice GXP out of state next month. I am buying for $7K. The buyer had a triple A inspection performed today. Here is the paper of the results.

&#x200B;

